course: dictionary
lesson: 6064
title: Merely: only, just, nothing more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Merely is an adverb that means only or just. It makes something sound small or not very important. «She merely smiled» means she smiled and did nothing more: no laugh, no speech, just a smile.
explain: Ask yourself: could I swap in just or only and keep the meaning? «It was merely a joke» means it was only a joke, nothing serious. Merely is a more formal word, so you will meet it in books and formal writing, while everyday speech usually says just or only.
explain: Sometimes merely shows that a number is small. «The team won merely 3 out of 30 games» tells you they won only a few. Look at the words around merely: they show you how small the thing is.
fact: Merely means only or just.
fact: Merely is a formal word; everyday speech usually says just or only.
fact: Merely can mean nothing more than, as in «It was merely a joke»: it was nothing more than a joke.
fact: Watch for the phrase merely a, as in «merely a small thing».
fact: To check a meaning, swap in just or only; if the sentence still makes sense, merely means the same.
exercise: «She merely smiled when we asked her to sing.» What does merely mean here? | Angrily, with a frown | *Only or just, with no more than a smile | Loudly and happily | Ten times over
exercise: Ellie says, «It was merely a joke.» What does merely tell us? | It was a serious plan | It was a very long story | It was a big surprise | *It was nothing more than a joke
exercise: «The team won merely 3 out of 30 games.» What does merely show? | *The team won only a few, a small number | The team did not play at all | The team won every game | The team won a lot of games
test: «He is merely a beginner, so be patient with him.» What does merely mean? | *Only, just a beginner | Very famous | Angry and loud | Lost in the rain
test: Which word could replace merely in «She merely smiled» without changing the meaning? | Sadly | *Just | Loudly | Quickly
exam: Which word means the same as merely in «It was merely a joke»? | *Only | Loudly | Slowly | Angrily
